60,3,1,talk,Mio,,"@Toshio, Option C is a nightmare for your six hours of chasing. Option A is too scary for the quiet ones. I think B is the only way to balance this.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"@Mio, I agree. Option B offers a gentle entry point. It lets newcomers start small, reducing the fear of a large, opaque commitment.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Mio, Option B is a trap. It lets the eighteen ghosts pay the minimum and vanish. I need a structure that forces the same nine to stop drowning.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, Option C is the only one that keeps the treasurer sane. Chasing individual event payments is manageable; chasing annual arrears is not.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Takumi, I hear your frustration, but Option B lets the quiet ones start small. If they feel safe, they might actually stay and help.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Mio,,"@Takumi, I hear you, but Option B lets people choose their level. If they pick the active tier, they commit to showing up. It’s a fair trade.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Takumi, I understand the frustration. But forcing participation often breeds resentment. Option B allows people to opt into a level they can genuinely sustain, which might actually encourage them to stay.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Mio, choosing a level doesn't make them show up. The ghosts will pick the lowest tier and disappear. I need a structure that demands presence, not just payment.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, forcing presence is a different problem. Option C keeps the books clean. If they don't pay for the event, they don't attend. No chasing.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Toshio, I worry Option C makes every event feel like a transaction. That visibility might scare the quiet ones away before they even start.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Mio,,"@Kenji, I get that, but Option B lets them pick a low tier without the scary upfront bill. It’s safer than C’s constant transactions.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Kenji, I agree. Option B feels less like a transaction and more like a choice. It respects the quiet ones' need for space while still offering a path to deeper involvement.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Kenji, ""safe"" is just a polite word for ""ignored."" If the quiet ones stay invisible, the active ones burn out. I need a structure that demands contribution, not just comfort.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, demanding contribution is a rota problem, not a fee one. Option C keeps the books clean. If they don't pay, they don't attend. No chasing.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"@Takumi, I hear you, but if we make every event a transaction, the quiet ones will feel like outsiders. Option B lets them belong without the pressure of a big bill.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Mio,,"I am voting for B. It balances the fixed costs with a gentle entry point, protecting both the treasurer and the newcomers.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I am voting for B. It offers a gentle entry point that reduces the fear of a large, opaque commitment, allowing people to belong without anxiety.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I am voting for C. It is the only option that stops the ghosts from paying and vanishing. If you do not pay, you do not attend.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"I am voting for C. It is the only option that protects the treasurer from the six-hour monthly burden. Clean books, no chasing.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Kenji,,"I am voting for B. It lets the quiet ones start small and feel safe, so they stay and eventually find their feet without the pressure of a big bill.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
